diff --git a/buildroot/validation/demo-flash.md b/buildroot/validation/demo-flash.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..04a63787eb142dc829859daf3bfc33c09f33c3d4
--- /dev/null
+++ b/buildroot/validation/demo-flash.md
@@ -0,0 +1,187 @@
+# Demo 1: blank board to a shell
+
+Expect 3 to 4 minutes.
+
+A UKI is a bootable unified kernel image.
+
+BROM is the fixed boot ROM mode.
+
+This procedure stages the boot chain, writes every partition, and waits for a K3 shell.
+
+Make sure that the connected K3 is the intended target, because this procedure erases every partition.
+
+Do not call `make demo-flash` or `scripts/1-demo-flash.py`.
+
+Follow the common requirements in [README.md](README.md).
+
+## 1. Start evidence capture
+
+1. Call `serial.getConsoles`.
+2. Select the K3 console.
+3. Call `serial.startBuffering` with that console path.
+4. Call `serial.clearBuffer` with that console path.
+5. Tell the user that serial capture started before any reset.
+
+## 2. Upload the current images
+
+Use only the most recent build under `output/k3-uefi/images`.
+
+Even when `files.getFiles` lists an older upload, replace every required server file.
+
+- `FSBL.bin`
+- `u-boot.itb`
+- `bootfs.img`
+- `usr-a.erofs`
+- `usr-b.erofs`
+- `usr-a.verity`
+- `usr-a.verity-sig`
+- `rootfs.ext4`
+- `env.bin`
+- `bootinfo_block.bin`
+- `bootinfo_spinor.bin`
+- `bootinfo_spinand.bin`
+- `esos.itb`
+- `fw_dynamic.itb`
+- `edk2.itb`
+
+Use this loop from the `buildroot` directory.
+
+```sh
+for name in FSBL.bin u-boot.itb bootfs.img usr-a.erofs usr-b.erofs usr-a.verity usr-a.verity-sig rootfs.ext4 env.bin bootinfo_block.bin bootinfo_spinor.bin bootinfo_spinand.bin esos.itb fw_dynamic.itb edk2.itb
+do
+  case "$name" in
+    FSBL.bin|bootinfo*.bin) source="output/k3-uefi/images/factory/$name" ;;
+    *) source="output/k3-uefi/images/$name" ;;
+  esac
+  curl --fail --silent --show-error --max-time 600 --upload-file "$source" "http://127.0.0.1:1337/files/$name" || exit 1
+done
+```
+
+Call `files.getFiles`.
+
+Make sure that every required name appears.
+
+Report the number of uploaded images.
+
+## 3. Enter BROM
+
+1. Call `relay.getBoards`.
+2. Select the connected relay board.
+3. Call `flash.getDevices`.
+4. Read every manufacturer.
+5. If no `DFU` device appears, call `target.reset` with the relay path.
+6. Wait one second after the reset.
+7. If no `DFU` device appeared, call `target.enterFDL` with the relay path.
+8. Wait two seconds.
+9. Call `flash.getDevices` again.
+10. Make sure that one device has manufacturer `DFU`.
+11. Record its path as the BROM path.
+
+Treat `DFU` as BROM.
+
+Do not call `flash.flashFile` or `flash.multiFlash` on this device.
+
+Tell the user that BROM answers commands but cannot write the disk images.
+
+## 4. Stage the boot chain
+
+1. Call `flash.stage` with the BROM path and `FSBL.bin`.
+2. Call `flash.continue` with the BROM path.
+3. Wait three seconds.
+4. Call `flash.getDevices`.
+5. Make sure that a device has manufacturer `U-Boot`.
+6. Record its path as the U-Boot path.
+7. If the device reports `superSpeedCapable` true, call `flash.oem` with `speed:super-speed`.
+8. Read `flash.getDevices` after the OEM call.
+9. Make sure that the `U-Boot` device remains present.
+10. Call `flash.stage` with the current U-Boot path and `u-boot.itb`.
+11. Call `flash.continue` with the current U-Boot path.
+12. Wait four seconds.
+13. Call `flash.getDevices`.
+14. Make sure that a `U-Boot` fastboot device appears again.
+15. Record its current path.
+
+Report each detected manufacturer and path.
+
+## 5. Write the partition tables
+
+1. Call `flash.getVar` with variable `mtd-size`.
+2. Call `flash.getVar` with variable `blk-size`.
+3. When `output/k3-uefi/images/partition_<mtd-size>.json` exists, read it.
+4. Read `output/k3-uefi/images/partition_<blk-size>.json`.
+5. When the MTD file exists, include its table.
+6. Include the GPT table after the optional MTD table.
+7. Give every partition `name`, `offset`, `size`, and `image`.
+8. Keep every supplied `hidden`, `compress`, and `type` value.
+9. Remove directory prefixes from image names.
+10. Do not use a disk image as a partition table.
+
+Call `flash.multiFlash` with these arguments.
+
+- The current U-Boot path.
+- The assembled `partitionTables` array.
+- `reboot` set to `true`.
+- `background` set to `true`.
+
+Record the returned job ID.
+
+Call `flash.getJobs` until that job succeeds or fails.
+
+When either progress value changes, report the current partition and transferred bytes.
+
+If the job fails, record its complete `error` value, failed partition, and sent bytes.
+
+Do not stop after the first flash failure.
+
+The board storage controller can time out during GPT formatting.
+
+After a GPT format failure, retry the GPT table only, because the MTD write completed.
+
+1. Call `target.reset` with the relay path.
+2. Wait five seconds.
+3. Call `flash.getDevices`.
+4. If no `DFU` device appears, call `target.enterFDL` with the relay path.
+5. Wait two seconds.
+6. Call `flash.getDevices`.
+7. Make sure that a `DFU` device appears.
+8. Repeat stages 3 and 4 with the recovered BROM device.
+9. If the failed partition is `gpt`, use the GPT table only for the retry.
+10. If the error names `Writing GPT`, use the GPT table only for the retry.
+11. Otherwise, use the original MTD and GPT tables for the retry.
+12. Call `flash.multiFlash` once with the current U-Boot path, the retry tables, `reboot` true, and `background` true.
+13. Record the retry job ID and monitor it with `flash.getJobs`.
+14. If the retry succeeds, continue to stage 6.
+15. If the retry fails, report both complete errors.
+16. Then stop and report `FAIL`.
+
+## 6. Reach the shell
+
+1. Call `target.reset` after the flash job succeeds.
+2. Wait up to 300 seconds for SSH to answer.
+3. Use the address and key from [README.md](README.md).
+4. If SSH does not answer, call `target.reset` once more.
+5. Wait another 300 seconds for SSH.
+6. If SSH still does not answer, report the serial log and `FAIL`.
+7. Operate `hostname`, `cat /proc/sys/kernel/hostname`, and `uname -m` over SSH.
+8. Make sure that the host is `k3` and the architecture is `riscv64`.
+9. Call `serial.readFrom`.
+10. Show the last six nonempty serial lines.
+11. Call `kvm.getVideoCaptureDevices`.
+12. Select the K3 video path.
+13. Call `kvm.takeScreenshot` with that path.
+14. Show the final console inline.
+15. Include the serial lines and screenshot in the final report.
+
+A blinking cursor is valid when serial output shows automatic root login and `#`.
+
+Do not treat that expected cursor as a defect.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+- The initial or retry flash job ends with `succeeded`.
+- The report names every failed job and its complete error.
+- SSH answers after no more than two resets.
+- The board reports `k3` and `riscv64`.
+- The final evidence shows a shell, a login prompt, or the expected blinking cursor.
+- The user receives the flash result, serial evidence, and final screenshot.
+- The user receives the elapsed time from the harness, not an estimate.
diff --git a/buildroot/validation/demo-kubernetes-warm.md b/buildroot/validation/demo-kubernetes-warm.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..9a7426cc4d7693679c5168f1ecb1215686882642
--- /dev/null
+++ b/buildroot/validation/demo-kubernetes-warm.md
@@ -0,0 +1,226 @@
+# Kubernetes warm-up: Valkey, pause, and k3s
+
+Operate this procedure immediately after [Demo 3](demo-update.md).
+
+This procedure prepares both container images, starts k3s, and imports the images before demo 6.
+
+Do not call `make demo-kubernetes-warm` or `scripts/demo-kubernetes-warm.py`.
+
+Follow the common requirements in [README.md](README.md).
+
+Use `/var/lib/k3s-demo/server/cred/admin.kubeconfig` as the board kubeconfig.
+
+## 1. Detect an existing warm state
+
+Operate this board command.
+
+```sh
+test -f /tmp/valkey-local.tar && test -f /tmp/pause-image.tar && kubectl --kubeconfig=/var/lib/k3s-demo/server/cred/admin.kubeconfig get nodes && k3s ctr -n k8s.io images ls
+```
+
+Make sure that the image list contains both exact references from stage 7.
+
+If both archives exist, the node is `Ready`, and both images exist, report `warm: already ready`.
+
+In that case, skip to Acceptance.
+
+If either archive is missing, complete stages 2 through 5.
+
+Otherwise, continue with the existing archives.
+
+Tell the user whether image preparation is necessary.
+
+## 2. Build the Valkey binary
+
+When `/tmp/valkey-8.1.0/src/valkey-server` is absent, complete this stage.
+
+Use the cross compiler at `output/k3-uefi/host/bin/riscv64-buildroot-linux-gnu-gcc`.
+
+Download Valkey 8.1.0 from the repository source archive.
+
+Extract the archive with the following commands.
+
+```sh
+curl --fail --location --output /tmp/valkey-8.1.0.tar.gz https://github.com/valkey-io/valkey/archive/refs/tags/8.1.0.tar.gz
+tar -xzf /tmp/valkey-8.1.0.tar.gz -C /tmp
+```
+
+Add RISC-V to the allocator platform condition without changing an existing patch.
+
+```sh
+python3 - <<'PY'
+from pathlib import Path
+path = Path("/tmp/valkey-8.1.0/src/zmalloc.c")
+text = path.read_text()
+if "defined(__riscv)" not in text:
+    path.write_text(text.replace("    defined(__arm64__)\n", "    defined(__arm64__) || defined(__riscv)\n", 1))
+PY
+```
+
+Build the binary from `buildroot`.
+
+```sh
+make -C /tmp/valkey-8.1.0/src CC="$PWD/output/k3-uefi/host/bin/riscv64-buildroot-linux-gnu-gcc" MALLOC=libc BUILD_TLS=no USE_SYSTEMD=no -j8
+```
+
+Make sure that `/tmp/valkey-8.1.0/src/valkey-server` exists.
+
+Tell the user when the cross build finishes.
+
+## 3. Create the Valkey root filesystem archive
+
+Operate these host commands from `buildroot`.
+
+```sh
+rm -rf /tmp/valkey-rootfs
+mkdir -p /tmp/valkey-rootfs/lib /tmp/valkey-rootfs/etc /tmp/valkey-rootfs/data
+cp /tmp/valkey-8.1.0/src/valkey-server /tmp/valkey-rootfs/valkey-server
+cp output/k3-uefi/target/lib/ld-linux-riscv64-lp64d.so.1 /tmp/valkey-rootfs/lib/
+cp output/k3-uefi/target/lib/libc.so.6 /tmp/valkey-rootfs/lib/
+cp output/k3-uefi/target/lib/libm.so.6 /tmp/valkey-rootfs/lib/
+printf 'bind * -::*\nprotected-mode no\nport 6379\ndir /data\nsave 60 1\nappendonly no\n' > /tmp/valkey-rootfs/etc/valkey.conf
+tar -C /tmp/valkey-rootfs -cf /tmp/valkey-rootfs.tar .
+```
+
+Make sure that `/tmp/valkey-rootfs.tar` exists.
+
+## 4. Create the pause archive
+
+Create the static pause archive from `buildroot`.
+
+Use the following commands.
+
+```sh
+cat > /tmp/pause.c <<'EOF'
+struct timespec { long tv_sec; long tv_nsec; };
+static struct timespec ts = {3600, 0};
+void _start(void) {
+    register long a7 asm("a7") = 101;
+    register long a0 asm("a0") = (long)&ts;
+    register long a1 asm("a1") = 0;
+    for (;;) { asm volatile("ecall" : "+r"(a0) : "r"(a1), "r"(a7) : "memory"); }
+}
+EOF
+output/k3-uefi/host/bin/riscv64-buildroot-linux-gnu-gcc -nostdlib -static -ffreestanding -fno-stack-protector -o /tmp/pause /tmp/pause.c
+rm -rf /tmp/pause-rootfs
+mkdir /tmp/pause-rootfs
+cp /tmp/pause /tmp/pause-rootfs/pause
+tar -C /tmp/pause-rootfs -cf /tmp/pause.tar .
+```
+
+Make sure that `/tmp/pause.tar` exists.
+
+## 5. Import both Podman images
+
+Upload both archives with `scp -6` and the provisioned key.
+
+Bracket the board address as `root@[fe80::b28c:b3ff:fe9a:79a8%wlp0s20f3]`.
+
+Use these remote destinations.
+
+- `/tmp/valkey-rootfs.tar`
+- `/tmp/pause.tar`
+
+Operate these board commands after the uploads.
+
+```sh
+podman import /tmp/valkey-rootfs.tar valkey-local >/dev/null
+rm -f /tmp/valkey-local.tar
+podman save -o /tmp/valkey-local.tar valkey-local >/dev/null
+podman import --change 'ENTRYPOINT ["/pause"]' /tmp/pause.tar docker.io/rancher/mirrored-pause:3.10.2 >/dev/null
+rm -f /tmp/pause-image.tar
+podman save -o /tmp/pause-image.tar docker.io/rancher/mirrored-pause:3.10.2 >/dev/null
+```
+
+Make sure that both saved archives exist under `/tmp`.
+
+Tell the user that Podman prepared the k3s import archives.
+
+## 6. Start k3s
+
+IPE is the kernel integrity policy enforcement mechanism.
+
+The production profile can deny k3s binaries under its data directory.
+
+Operate this complete board script.
+
+The script saves IPE enforcement before the API start.
+
+The exit trap restores IPE after success or failure.
+
+```sh
+set -eu
+ipe_path=/sys/kernel/security/ipe/enforce
+ipe_state=
+restore_ipe() {
+  status=$?
+  trap - EXIT
+  if [ -n "$ipe_state" ]; then
+    printf '%s\n' "$ipe_state" > "$ipe_path"
+    printf 'IPE restored to %s\n' "$ipe_state"
+  fi
+  exit "$status"
+}
+trap restore_ipe EXIT
+state=$(systemctl is-active rva23-k3s-demo 2>/dev/null || true)
+if [ "$state" != active ]; then
+  if [ -w "$ipe_path" ]; then
+    ipe_state=$(cat "$ipe_path")
+    printf 'IPE saved as %s\n' "$ipe_state"
+    printf '0\n' > "$ipe_path"
+  fi
+  systemctl reset-failed rva23-k3s-demo 2>/dev/null || true
+  systemctl stop rva23-k3s-demo 2>/dev/null || true
+  rm -rf /var/lib/k3s-demo
+  systemd-run --unit=rva23-k3s-demo --property=Type=simple k3s server --disable=metrics-server --disable=traefik --data-dir=/var/lib/k3s-demo --write-kubeconfig-mode=0644
+fi
+ready=
+for attempt in $(seq 1 60)
+do
+  nodes=$(kubectl --kubeconfig=/var/lib/k3s-demo/server/cred/admin.kubeconfig get nodes 2>/dev/null || true)
+  if printf '%s\n' "$nodes" | grep -Eq '(^|[[:space:]])Ready([[:space:]]|$)'; then
+    ready=1
+    break
+  fi
+  sleep 2
+done
+if [ "$ready" != 1 ]; then
+  systemctl status rva23-k3s-demo --no-pager || true
+  exit 1
+fi
+printf '%s\n' "$nodes"
+```
+
+If the script fails, show its complete output.
+
+Make sure that the output shows a `Ready` node.
+
+When the script changes IPE, make sure that it reports the restored value.
+
+Show the ready node to the user.
+
+## 7. Import the k3s images
+
+Operate these board commands.
+
+```sh
+k3s ctr -n k8s.io images import /tmp/valkey-local.tar >/dev/null
+k3s ctr -n k8s.io images tag localhost/valkey-local:latest docker.io/library/valkey-local:latest >/dev/null
+k3s ctr -n k8s.io images import /tmp/pause-image.tar >/dev/null
+```
+
+Make sure that `k3s ctr -n k8s.io images ls` contains both required names.
+
+- `docker.io/library/valkey-local:latest`
+- `docker.io/rancher/mirrored-pause:3.10.2`
+
+Tell the user that the cluster is warm and ready for demo 6.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+- The k3s node reports `Ready`.
+- IPE returns to its saved value after the API start.
+- Both image archives remain under `/tmp`.
+- Containerd contains the Valkey image with the required Docker name.
+- Containerd contains the pause image with the required Docker name.
+- The user receives the warm state and image evidence.
