@@ -0,0 +1,168 @@
+# Demo 6: Valkey on k3s and signed extension rejection
+
+Expect 30 to 60 seconds after the warm-up.
+
+RESP is the Valkey wire protocol.
+
+This procedure starts a Valkey pod, tests RESP over IPv6, and rejects a modified k3s system extension.
+
+Complete [Demo 3](demo-update.md) and [Kubernetes warm-up](demo-kubernetes-warm.md) first.
+
+Do not call `make demo-kubernetes` or `scripts/6-demo-kubernetes.py`.
+
+Follow the common requirements in [README.md](README.md).
+
+Use `/var/lib/k3s-demo/server/cred/admin.kubeconfig` as the board kubeconfig.
+
+## 1. Make sure that the cluster is warm
+
+1. Operate `systemctl is-active rva23-k3s-demo` over SSH.
+2. If the state is not `active`, report that the warm-up is incomplete.
+3. In that case, stop.
+4. Operate `systemctl reset-failed rva23-k3s-demo 2>/dev/null || true` over SSH.
+5. Allow up to 180 seconds for the node `STATUS` column to equal `Ready`.
+6. Use `kubectl --kubeconfig=/var/lib/k3s-demo/server/cred/admin.kubeconfig get nodes` for each probe.
+7. If the node stays unready, show `systemctl status rva23-k3s-demo --no-pager`.
+8. In that case, report `FAIL`.
+9. Show the ready node to the user.
+
+## 2. Start the Valkey pod
+
+Write this manifest to `/tmp/valkey-k3s.yaml` on the board.
+
+```yaml
+apiVersion: v1
+kind: Pod
+metadata:
+  name: valkey-k3s
+spec:
+  hostNetwork: true
+  containers:
+    - name: valkey
+      image: docker.io/library/valkey-local:latest
+      imagePullPolicy: Never
+      command:
+        - /lib/ld-linux-riscv64-lp64d.so.1
+        - --library-path
+        - /lib
+        - /valkey-server
+        - /etc/valkey.conf
+      args:
+        - --port
+        - "6380"
+```
+
+Operate these board commands.
+
+```sh
+kubectl --kubeconfig=/var/lib/k3s-demo/server/cred/admin.kubeconfig apply -f /tmp/valkey-k3s.yaml
+kubectl --kubeconfig=/var/lib/k3s-demo/server/cred/admin.kubeconfig wait --for=condition=Ready pod/valkey-k3s --timeout=120s
+```
+
+Make sure that the wait output contains `condition met`.
+
+Tell the user that the pod uses host networking and port 6380.
+
+## 3. Test RESP over IPv6
+
+Operate this Python command on the laptop.
+
+```sh
+python3 - <<'PY'
+import socket
+
+sock = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET6, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
+sock.settimeout(6)
+sock.connect(("fe80::b28c:b3ff:fe9a:79a8", 6380, 0, socket.if_nametoindex("wlp0s20f3")))
+try:
+    for command, expected in ((b"PING\r\n", b"+PONG"), (b"SET demo:verify ok\r\n", b"+OK"), (b"GET demo:verify\r\n", b"ok")):
+        sock.sendall(command)
+        response = sock.recv(100)
+        print(response.decode().strip())
+        if expected not in response:
+            raise RuntimeError(f"unexpected response: {response!r}")
+finally:
+    sock.close()
+PY
+```
+
+Make sure that the output contains `PONG`, `OK`, and `ok`.
+
+Show those responses to the user.
+
+## 4. Reject a modified k3s extension
+
+Tell the user that one byte will change inside the signed k3s extension.
+
+Operate this complete board script.
+
+The script stops when backup creation fails.
+
+The exit trap restores the backup and remerges it after every outcome.
+
+```sh
+set -eu
+image=$(find /var/lib/extensions -maxdepth 1 -name 'k3s_*.raw' -not -name '*.backup' | sort -V | tail -n 1)
+test -n "$image"
+test -f "$image"
+backup="$image.backup"
+restore_image() {
+  status=$?
+  trap - EXIT
+  systemd-sysext unmerge >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
+  if [ -f "$backup" ]; then
+    mv -f "$backup" "$image"
+  fi
+  systemd-sysext --image-policy=root=signed+absent:usr=signed+absent refresh
+  exit "$status"
+}
+trap restore_image EXIT
+systemd-sysext unmerge
+rm -f "$backup"
+cp "$image" "$backup"
+printf X | dd of="$image" bs=1 seek=1048576 conv=notrunc 2>/dev/null
+set +e
+output=$(SYSTEMD_LOG_LEVEL=debug systemd-sysext --image-policy=root=signed+absent:usr=signed+absent refresh 2>&1)
+status=$?
+set -e
+printf '%s\n' "$output"
+if [ "$status" -eq 0 ]; then
+  printf 'ERROR: the modified image was accepted\n'
+  exit 1
+fi
+printf '%s\n' "$output" | grep -E 'k3s_[^ ]*\.raw'
+printf '%s\n' "$output" | grep -Ei 'signature|verity|authentication|bad message|input/output error'
+printf 'The modified extension was rejected: %s\n' "$image"
+```
+
+Make sure that the refresh output names the modified k3s image.
+
+Make sure that the error indicates a signature or verity authentication failure.
+
+If the refresh succeeds, make sure that the script returns nonzero after restoration.
+
+If any step fails, show the complete output and restoration result.
+
+Make sure that the exit trap successfully remerges the original image.
+
+Tell the user that the modified extension was rejected and the original extension merged again.
+
+## 5. Show the final cluster state
+
+Operate these board commands.
+
+```sh
+systemd-sysext status --no-pager
+kubectl --kubeconfig=/var/lib/k3s-demo/server/cred/admin.kubeconfig get pods -o wide
+```
+
+Make sure that the k3s extension appears under `/usr` and `valkey-k3s` is `Running`.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+- The k3s node and Valkey pod report `Ready`.
+- IPv6 RESP returns `PONG`, `OK`, and `ok` on port 6380.
+- The signed refresh rejects the one-byte modification.
+- The restored k3s extension merges successfully.
+- The final pod state is `Running`.
+- The user receives the RESP responses, rejection error, and final cluster state.
